        <h2>Why Templates Fall Short on Performance</h2>
        <p>Page speed is not a nice-to-have. It is a ranking factor, a conversion factor, and a trust factor. Google has made this clear with Core Web Vitals: your site needs to load fast, respond quickly to user input, and stay stable as it loads. Templates struggle with all three.</p>
        <p>The problem is bloat. Templates are built to appeal to the widest possible audience. That means they include dozens of features, scripts, and styles you will never use. All that unused code still loads. It still slows your site down. It still hurts your scores.</p>
        <p>Studies show that 47% of users bounce if a page takes longer than 2 seconds to load. Template-based sites often average 4 to 8 seconds. That is not a small gap. That is the difference between capturing a lead and losing a prospect before they even see your offer. Custom sites, by contrast, are built lean. Only the code you need is included. Every asset is optimized. The result is faster load times, better user experience, and higher search rankings.</p>
        <p>Mobile performance is even worse for templates. Many templates drop 30% or more in mobile speed scores compared to desktop. In 2026, mobile traffic dominates. If your site is slow on phones, you are leaving money on the table.</p>

        <h2>SEO: The Gap Is Getting Wider</h2>
        <p>Search engine optimization in 2026 is more technical than ever. Google evaluates hundreds of signals, and many of them happen at the code level. Site structure, heading hierarchy, <a href="/services/schema-markup">schema markup</a>, internal linking, page speed, mobile usability, and crawlability all matter. Templates give you limited control over most of these.</p>
        <p>Template code often uses improper heading structures. H1 tags end up in sidebars. Multiple H1s appear on a single page. Schema markup is missing or generic. Internal linking is rigid. These are not minor issues. They are fundamental SEO problems that directly affect whether Google understands and ranks your content.</p>
        <p>Custom websites are built with SEO as a foundation, not an afterthought. Clean code, proper semantic structure, fast load times, and flexible schema markup all give you an edge. Businesses with custom sites typically see 20% to 40% better organic visibility than those on templates. In competitive markets, that gap decides who gets the call and who does not.</p>
        <p>AI search is also changing the game. Tools like ChatGPT and Perplexity pull answers from well-structured, fast-loading, authoritative sites. Templates do not give you the control you need to show up in these new search formats. Custom builds do.</p>

        <h2>Conversion Rate: Where Custom Sites Pull Ahead</h2>
        <p>A website that looks good but does not convert is just an expensive brochure. The whole point of a business website is to turn visitors into leads and leads into customers. Templates are not designed for your specific customer journey. They are designed to look acceptable to the widest possible range of buyers.</p>
        <p>Custom websites are built around conversion. The placement of every call-to-action, the flow from homepage to contact form, the messaging on each page, the social proof positioning — all of it is intentional. It is based on your audience, your offer, and your sales process. That is why custom sites routinely achieve 2 to 3x higher conversion rates than template sites.</p>
        <p>Templates also make A/B testing and iteration difficult. Want to move a form above the fold? Add a trust badge? Change the navigation structure? On a template, you are fighting the layout. On a custom site, you make the change, test it, and improve. That agility compounds over time.</p>
        <p>Here is a simple example. A local home services company switches from a template to a custom site with a conversion-focused design. Their contact form submissions go from 4 per month to 18 per month. At an average job value of $2,000, that is an extra $28,000 per month in pipeline. The custom site paid for itself in the first month.</p>

        <h2>The Hidden Costs and Risks of Templates</h2>
        <p>Templates have costs that do not show up on the invoice. Let us walk through the ones that hurt business owners the most.</p>
        <p><strong>Vendor lock-in.</strong> Platforms like Wix and Squarespace own your site infrastructure. You cannot export your design and move it somewhere else. If they raise prices, change their terms, or experience downtime, you have no recourse. Your business is tied to their platform forever unless you rebuild.</p>
        <p><strong>Security vulnerabilities.</strong> Popular templates are used by thousands of sites. That makes them attractive targets for hackers. When a vulnerability is discovered, every site using that template is at risk. Custom code is proprietary. It is not on any hacker's target list.</p>
        <p><strong>Plugin dependency.</strong> Templates often rely on third-party plugins for basic functionality. When a plugin developer abandons their project, your site breaks. We have seen businesses lose contact forms, booking systems, and even entire pages because a free plugin stopped being maintained.</p>
        <p><strong>Lost differentiation.</strong> If your competitor is using the same $79 template, how do you stand out? You do not. In service-based industries where trust is everything, looking generic is a serious disadvantage. Customers judge credibility in seconds. A cookie-cutter site signals a cookie-cutter business.</p>
        <p><strong>Time cost.</strong> Business owners underestimate how long it takes to customize a template. What looks like a weekend project turns into a month of frustration. Your time has value. Every hour you spend wrestling with a template is an hour you are not spending on revenue-generating work.</p>

